Agent Response: Short answer: for official uses tied to your SSN (tax forms, employment verification, benefits, background checks, Social Security records) your name should match the name on your Social Security record exactly. Mismatches or even small spelling differences can cause verification failures, delays, or extra paperwork. For informal/internal forms it may be less strict.

What to do and next steps
- Check the form’s instructions — many forms explicitly say “use your legal name as shown on Social Security card.”  
- If the spelling on the document differs from your Social Security name:
  - Ask the organization whether they require the exact SSA spelling or will accept a minor variation. If they require a match, they will usually tell you what documentation they need.
  - If needed, provide supporting documentation (copy of Social Security card, marriage certificate, court order) as requested.
  - If your SSA record is incorrect or outdated, contact the Social Security Administration to correct it (visit ssa.gov or call 1-800-772-1213). SSA lists required documents for name changes/updates on their site.
- For employers/taxes: make sure the name on your W-4 / payroll record matches SSA to avoid W-2/IRS mismatches.
- If you’re asked only for the last four digits of your SSN, that’s safer to provide than the whole SSN; avoid sharing the full SSN publicly or in insecure channels.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email you can send asking the agency whether an exact match is required (I can include a template below).  
- Help you draft the documentation request or the SSA name-change steps.

Quick template you can use to ask the organization
Subject: Question about name spelling and SSN verification for [Form Name]
Body:
Hi [Name or Team],
I’m completing [form name]. The name I use on this form is spelled “[spelling on form],” but my legal name as it appears on my Social Security card is “[your legal name].” Will the discrepancy cause any verification issues? If so, please let me know what documentation you need or how you’d like me to proceed.
Thanks,  
[Your name]

Would you like me to draft/send that email for you (and should I avoid including your full SSN)?
