I noticed a small inconsistency in your message: you wrote "XXX-XX-2345" and also "last four: 6789." Which last-four is correct? (You don't need to repeat the full SSN — just confirm which four digits are meant.)

Short answer
- In most cases your name on the document should match exactly the name on file with the Social Security Administration (SSA). If it doesn’t, the form may be delayed or flagged for verification.

Why exact match matters
- Agencies and employers often verify the name + SSN against SSA records. Differences (e.g., missing/mismatched middle name or initial, different order, or a different last name) can cause a mismatch even if the SSN is correct.
- Government forms (tax, benefits, DMV, SSA) are strict about legal name. Financial institutions and employers may also require the legal name for payroll/tax reporting.
- Small formatting differences (periods, commas, diacritics) are usually tolerated, but substantive differences (legal last name vs. nickname, omitted middle name when SSA has it, or an outdated surname) are more likely to cause problems.

What to do now
1. Check the form’s instructions — some forms explicitly say “enter name exactly as shown on Social Security card” or allow “also known as” entries.
2. If possible, enter the name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card. If your name has changed (marriage, divorce, etc.), you should update your SSA record first.
3. If you cannot enter the exact SSA name:
   - Use the legal name you have on your SSN card if you can access it.
   - Add a parenthetical or note if the form permits (e.g., “Jane M. Doe (formerly Jane M. Smith)” or “aka Jane Smith”) and attach supporting documentation if requested.
   - For electronic forms with validation errors, contact the form issuer (HR, bank support, agency helpdesk) and explain the discrepancy — they can advise next steps.
4. If the form is for employment/tax purposes: contact payroll or HR before submitting. They often have a process to resolve name/SSN mismatches.
5. If you get a rejection or delay, SSA can confirm the exact name on file. If that name is wrong, you must bring proof of identity and documentation to SSA to correct it.

When to correct your SSA record
- If your legal name has changed or the name on your SSA record is incorrect, update SSA as soon as possible. You’ll need identity documents and legal evidence of the name change (marriage certificate, court order, etc.).

Privacy reminder
- Avoid sharing your full SSN in email or public messages. If you must provide it to an organization, use their secure submission channels.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a short message to HR or the agency explaining the name/SSN mismatch (without including the full SSN).
- Look up SSA guidance wording if you tell me which type of form this is (tax, employer, bank, government benefits).

