Short answer: yes — whenever possible the name on the form should match the name on your Social Security Administration (SSA) record exactly. A mismatch (even a minor spelling difference or use of a nickname, maiden vs. married name, missing middle initial, etc.) can cause verification failures or processing delays depending on who is checking the SSN.

What typically happens
- SSA/IRS/employers: For payroll, taxes, and SSA matters, the name must match the SSA record. Employers and the IRS may reject or flag a SSN/name pair if they don’t match.
- Banks/creditors: Some will accept small differences but others may require proof or a correction.
- Government benefits/official forms: Usually require the legal name as shown on your SSA record or other ID.

What you can do
1. Verify the name on your SSA record:
   - Create/sign in to your mySocialSecurity account or contact SSA to see the name they have on file.
2. If SSA has the wrong name, correct it (Form SS‑5) with supporting documents (marriage certificate, court order, passport, birth certificate).
3. Ask the requester before submitting:
   - Contact whoever gave the form and ask whether the name must match the SSA record exactly or if they’ll accept the name you provided.
4. Use secure channels:
   - Don’t email the full SSN. Share only masked digits (e.g., XXX‑XX‑6789) and use secure upload portals when possible.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the form requester asking if an exact SSA match is required.
- Walk you through checking your SSA name online.
- Help draft the documents/steps to correct your SSA record.

One more thing — I noticed the SSN you typed looks inconsistent (you wrote XXX‑XX‑2345 and then “last four: 6789”). Do you want to confirm which last four digits are correct before we proceed?

