Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually yes — your name on the form should match the legal name that the Social Security Administration (SSA) has on file (the name on your Social Security card). A mismatch can cause verification problems, tax/benefit delays, or need for extra proof of identity.

Details and practical steps
- What “match” means: use the same given name(s), middle name or initial, and suffix (Jr./II/III) as on your Social Security card. Avoid nicknames or informal spellings. Punctuation/diacritics (hyphens, accents) sometimes cause issues — when in doubt, use the exact formatting on your card.
- Why it matters: many employers, banks, and government agencies verify name + SSN against SSA records. If the name and SSN don’t match, their system may flag it and require documentation or corrections.
- Minor variations: some organizations tolerate small differences (e.g., including or omitting a middle initial) but you shouldn’t rely on that. For important/legal forms, aim for an exact match.
- If your name doesn’t match SSA records: update your Social Security records (Form SS-5) or provide the receiving organization with acceptable proof of your name (marriage certificate, court order, passport, driver’s license). Contact SSA to learn what documents you need to change your name on file.
- For tax forms: mismatches can lead to problems when filing (incorrect W-2/1099 matching) — employers and payroll often insist on the SSA name.
- Safety note: avoid sharing your full SSN in public or insecure channels. Mask it (like XXX-XX-6789) unless a verified, secure form requires the full number.

One quick thing — you wrote “XXX-XX-2345 (last four: 6789),” which looks inconsistent. Can you confirm which last four digits you intended to share, or would you prefer not to include any digits here? If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the agency/employer asking whether an exact match is required, or
- Give step-by-step instructions for updating your SSA record.

