Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — use the exact legal name shown on your Social Security card (and other government ID) whenever a form ties your name to your SSN. Mismatches can cause verification failures or processing delays.

Why it matters
- The Social Security Administration (and systems that verify SSN/name pairs, including employers and the IRS) compares the name you provide to the name on SSA records. Spelling differences, missing/extra middle names or initials, or different suffixes can trigger a “name/SSN mismatch.”  
- A mismatch usually won’t be catastrophic, but it often creates delays: payroll/W‑2 problems, hold-ups in benefits enrollment, or requests for additional documentation.

Practical rules of thumb
- Enter your name exactly as it appears on your Social Security card whenever the form specifically asks for SSN or says “name as shown on Social Security card” or “legal name.”  
- Include suffixes (Jr., Sr., III) if they’re on your SSA record.  
- Middle name vs. middle initial: match what SSA has, if you can.  
- Hyphens, apostrophes, diacritics and punctuation sometimes cause system issues; match the SSA/card version as closely as possible.  
- If the form wants “full legal name” but your SSA record uses a different format, follow the form’s instruction and contact the organization (or SSA) to avoid problems.

If your name on the document doesn’t match
- If it’s a small typo, ask the recipient whether they will accept it; they may still be able to verify, but be prepared to provide ID or a corrected form.  
- If your legal name has changed (marriage, court order) and SSA hasn’t been updated, update your SSA record (Form SS‑5 or local SSA office) before submitting documents tied to the SSN.  
- For employer or tax forms, notify HR/payroll immediately so they can correct or advise — unresolved mismatches can affect tax reporting.

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a short note to the organization or HR explaining the situation and asking whether they need a corrected form or ID.  
- Give you wording to request an SSA name-change or to ask what documentation the recipient requires.

Finally — for your safety, avoid sharing the full SSN in email or chat. Masking the number (last 4 only) is the safer approach. Would you like me to draft a message to HR or the form recipient asking how they want you to proceed?

